<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oneida Lake</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> Lake is the largest lake lying wholly within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State, north-east of Syracuse. Its shores and the creeks flowing into it were the homeland of the Oneida nation of the Iroquois Confederacy, and it lay on the water route linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a> to Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. It later became part of the New York State canal system.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oneida-lake.jpg" alt="Oneida Lake" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joegrimes at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oneida_Lake_seen_from_Yacht_Club_in_Cicero_New_York.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oneida Lake has a surface area of 79.8 square miles (207 km2). It is about 21 miles (34 km) long and about 5 miles (8.0 km) wide, with an average depth of 22 feet (6.7 m). It is a remnant of Glacial Lake Iroquois, formed nearly 12,000 years ago when glaciers blocked the flow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a>; when the ice melted and that lake drained, water remained in a deeper basin of its former bed.</p><p>The lake drains through the Oneida River, a tributary of the Oswego River, into Lake Ontario. It is named for the Oneida, one of the Six Nations of the Iroquois, whose own name, Oneyoteaka, means People of the Standing Stone; they called the lake Tsioqui, meaning White Water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the 18th and early 19th centuries the lake and its tributary Wood Creek formed part of the Albany-Oswego waterway. Travellers went up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a>, crossed the portage of the Oneida Carry to Wood Creek, and left the lake by the Oneida River. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forced the Iroquois nations to cede most of their lands, and settlers built a canal with locks in Wood Creek; a canal cut across the Oneida Carry between 1792 and 1803 greatly increased shipping across the lake.</p><p>The opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> in 1825 bypassed the lake, and the communities around it lost their navigable route eastward. The old Oneida Canal, built in 1835 from Higginsville on the Erie Canal to Wood Creek, had poorly built wooden locks and closed in 1863. When the Erie Canal was rebuilt as the New York State Barge Canal in the early 20th century, its engine-powered barges could cross open water, so the new canalway entered the lake at Sylvan Beach and left it at Brewerton.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake is naturally eutrophic, rich in phosphorus and algae, and its shoals form disc-shaped iron and manganese nodules known as Oneida Lake pancakes. It borders Madison, Oneida, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> and Oswego counties and is fished chiefly for walleye, including through the ice in winter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oneida Lake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Oneida took shape as a nation in central New York around <b>Oneida Lake</b> and Oneida Creek, their name recalling a boulder that marked their principal settlement.</blockquote><a href="/state/oneida-nation">Oneida Nation</a></li></ul></section>
